 ## Overview
-Planar Gaussian Splatting (PGS) is an unsupervised neural rendering framework that reconstructs 3D geometry and explicitly parses 3D planar surfaces from multiple RGB images without requiring 3D labels or depth supervision [S1]. It extends the standard 3D Gaussian Splatting representation by introducing discriminative 3D plane descriptors and enforcing local planar alignment constraints [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that focus on high-fidelity rendering or generic mesh extraction, PGS is designed to identify distinct 3D plane instances through a hierarchical Gaussian Mixture Tree (GMT) [S1]. It sets itself apart by using 2D segmentation priors from foundation models (like SAM) to guide the learning of these descriptors, enabling robust planar reconstruction across different scenes [S1].
+Planar Gaussian Splatting (PGS) is an unsupervised neural rendering framework that reconstructs 3D geometry and explicitly parses 3D planar surfaces from multiple RGB images without requiring 3D labels or depth supervision [S1]. It extends the standard 3D Gaussian Splatting representation by introducing discriminative 3D plane descriptors and enforcing local planar alignment constraints [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that prioritize high-fidelity rendering or generic mesh extraction, PGS is designed to identify distinct 3D plane instances through a hierarchical Gaussian Mixture Tree [S1]. It sets itself apart by using 2D segmentation priors from foundation models like SAM to guide descriptor learning, enabling robust planar reconstruction across unseen scenes [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-PGS operates on a set of $N$ 3D Gaussians $\{G_i\}_{i=1}^N$. Each Gaussian $G_i$ retains the standard parameters: mean $\boldsymbol{\mu}_i$, covariance $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_i$ (derived from rotation $\mathbf{R}_i$ and scale $\mathbf{S}_i$), opacity $\alpha_i$, and view-dependent color $\mathbf{c}_i(\omega)$ [S1]. Additionally, each Gaussian carries two new parameters: a surface normal vector $\mathbf{n}_i$ and a learnable plane descriptor vector $\mathbf{z}_i$ [S1]. The descriptor $\mathbf{z}_i$ is constrained to unit norm ($\|\mathbf{z}_i\|_2 = 1$) and serves to identify distinct 3D planes [S1].
